{
  "id": "inlay_hints/parameter_hints/12_macros",
  "topic": "inlay_hints",
  "subtopic": "parameter_hints",
  "title": "Macros at call sites",
  "summary": "Arguments spelled as macros hint; calls generated inside macro bodies do not",
  "prose": "An object-like macro is one written argument. A macro covering several arguments has no place to anchor a hint, and code written as a macro argument keeps its hints, through a macro nested in it too, whose own body stays bare.",
  "status": "supported",
  "issues": [
    "clangd#2620"
  ],
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "main.cpp",
      "text": "void report(double value);\nvoid plot(double x, double y);\nint check(int status);\n\n#define PI 3.14\n#define CALL_REPORT() report(2.71)\n#define PAIR 1.0, 2.0\n#define ASSERT(expr) if(!(expr)) {}\n\nvoid outer_call(int first, int second, int third);\nint inner_call(int left, int right, int last);\n#define OUTER(arg) outer_call(0, 0, arg)\n#define INNER(arg) inner_call(0, 0, arg)\n#define SWAPPED(second, first) plot(first, second)\n\nvoid use() {\n    report(PI);\n    CALL_REPORT();\n    plot(PAIR);\n    ASSERT(check(42) == 0);\n    OUTER(INNER(3));\n    SWAPPED(4.0, 5.0);\n}\n",
